  python train.py --xml spiral_robot_v2.xml --timesteps 3000000 --n-envs 8 --run-name crawl_v1
1. 

Phase 0: Set up (about 1 hour)
Goal: the provided files run on a desktop.
1. Put spiral_robot.xml, baselink.stl and the six files from octopus_crawl/ in one folder.
2. pip install -r requirements.txt
3. python check_model.py --xml spiral_robot.xml
Exit check: the XML loads, and each muscle at full activation visibly bends the arm. If it warns that the arm barely moves, fix mass, muscle force or stiffness first.
Phase 1: Prove crawling is possible in sim (about 1 to 3 days)
Goal: a policy that crawls at all, using the files as they are.
1. Train: python train.py --xml spiral_robot.xml --timesteps 3000000 --n-envs 8 --run-name crawl_v1
2. Watch it in TensorBoard (tensorboard --logdir runs), then record: python evaluate.py --run runs/crawl_v1 --video crawl.mp4
3. If it fails, tune in this order: ground friction, muscle force, --timesteps, reward weights. Also check the video for exploits like flipping or launching.
Exit check: consistent forward motion across 5 evaluation episodes, no rolling over, and a gait that looks plausible rather than jittery.
This phase tells you whether the physical idea is feasible. If it can't crawl in sim with full access to the model, it won't on the real arm.
Phase 2: Decide the real arm's design from what sim tells you (about 1 day)
Goal: pick what the hardware needs.
1. Retrain once with the joint actuators removed from the XML, so only the two tendons act. See if it still crawls.
2. If it does, build the real arm with two tendon servos. If it doesn't, the real arm needs actuated joints or more tendons.
3. Measure the real arm's mass, length, and servo travel and torque, and start matching the XML to it.
Exit check: a sim model whose actuators match what you can build.
Phase 3: Make the sim match the hardware (new code needed, about 3 to 5 days)
Goal: a training setup the real arm can run.
1. Replace the observation with deployable signals only: servo encoders, base IMU, tip IMU, commanded positions, and history stacking.
2. Replace muscles with position-controlled tendon actuators.
3. Add sensor noise, 1 to 3 steps of latency, and domain randomization of friction, mass, stiffness and damping.
4. Add a heading command as an input, so the camera can steer later.
These are the pieces I haven't written yet. Ask me for them when you reach this phase.
Exit check: the policy still crawls in sim with randomization turned on and noisy sensors.
Phase 4: Calibrate the sim against the real arm (about 3 to 5 days, can overlap with Phase 3)
Goal: shrink the sim-to-real gap.
1. Do bend and drop tests on the real arm to get stiffness and damping.
2. Drag it across the surface to estimate friction.
3. Use an overhead camera with AprilTags to measure real positions.
4. Put these values into the XML and set the randomization ranges around them.
5. Retrain.
Exit check: a scripted sim motion and the same motion on the real arm look similar.
Phase 5: Deploy (about 2 to 4 days)
Goal: the policy runs on the arm.
1. Export the actor network and the VecNormalize statistics to ONNX or numpy. I'll write this when you get here.
2. Run a 50 Hz loop on the Jetson or the Pi: read sensors, normalize, run the network, clip, filter, send servo commands.
3. Test in stages: arm suspended with a torque cap, then open-loop replay of a sim trajectory, then the policy on the ground.
4. Keep an e-stop and hard limits on tendon travel through all of it.
Exit check: crawling on the real arm, even slowly.
Phase 6: Improve (optional)
* Add the camera on the Jetson to produce heading commands, so the arm steers toward a target.
* If the sim-to-real gap is small, try short, low-learning-rate fine-tuning on hardware, with action limits and an e-stop.
What you can do right now
Phase 0 and Phase 1 need nothing but the files you have. Start there, because everything later depends on whether the arm can crawl in sim. Send me the output of check_model.py and, once training finishes, the evaluation results or video notes, and I'll help you debug.
